      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the probability of two specific outcomes when rolling two six-sided dice: rolling a number less than three on the first die and rolling a number greater than three on the second die. We will use the concept of independent events and the fundamental principle of counting.\n\nFirst, let's identify the possible outcomes for each die:\n- A six-sided die has the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6.\n- A number less than three on the first die is either 1 or 2.\n- A number greater than three on the second die is either 4, 5, or 6.\n\nNext, we calculate the probability of each individual event:\n1. The probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{first die} < 3) = P(\\text{first die} = 1) + P(\\text{first die} = 2) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. The probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{second die} > 3) = P(\\text{second die} = 4) + P(\\text{second die} = 5) + P(\\text{second die} = 6) = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\nSince the two events (rolling a number less than three on the first die and rolling a number greater than three on the second die) are independent, we multiply their probabilities to find the combined probability:\n\\[\nP(\\text{first die} < 3 \\text{ and } \\text{second die} > 3) = P(\\text{first die} < 3) \\times P(\\text{second die} > 3) = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]",
