      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we can break down the problem into two independent events and then combine their probabilities.\n\n1. **Probability that the first die rolls a number less than three:**\n   A six-sided die has the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. The numbers less than three are 1 and 2. Therefore, there are 2 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{first die rolls less than 3}) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Probability that the second die rolls a number greater than three:**\n   The numbers greater than three are 4, 5, and 6. Therefore, there are 3 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{second die rolls greater than 3}) = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\nSince the two events are independent, the probability that both events occur (i.e., the first die rolls a number less than three and the second die rolls a number greater than three) is the product of the individual probabilities.\n\\[\nP(\\text{first die rolls less than 3 and second die rolls greater than 3}) = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
